# Installed serve case

Run this harness only on the authorized disposable Ubuntu box. Its default
installed-lifecycle mode now prepares a complete, explicit test-org publication
before calling the administrator installer. The box suite needs no additional
arguments for that input:

```sh
python3 -I -B scripts/v2-serve/run.py \
  --source /test/candidate \
  --evidence /test/evidence/lb-v2-serve-lifecycle
```

The existing root wrapper in e2e/v2/cases/serve/run.sh remains supported. The
default suite is lifecycle; --suite state uses the same publication and its
protected Codex launch proposal. These are declared software-authenticator and
vendor-executable fixtures. Presence remains simulated and human approval
remains not_proven. This is not an authenticated AI-service test.

The `--item-case grants` preparation includes the late-stop item in
`stopcont.py`. Its stand-in exits independently; the real Stop effect commits
the native exit during Observe preflight before StopGate. The item requires
a saved release fence with `stop arrived after natural exit`, an acknowledged
Codex refresh, no rotation, and a new L3 child on the same enrolled profile.
It retains `stop-before.input.json`, `stop-after.input.json`,
`stop-next.input.json` and `stopcont.json`. A running or uncommitted exit
continues to require rotation; the admin canaries exercise that twin.
Queue-supplied trees without Git metadata use `--source-snapshot`; their
source record says `git_revision: null` and preserves the actual source byte
inventory. Ordinary checkout runs still require the revision command.

The grants module leaves the initial lifecycle lanes on the ordinary Stop
path. Before declining the Codex normal-exit hook it verifies the selected
baseline profile and separate parent/child item profiles. The baseline's
running Stop must quarantine that canonical seat: `item.grants.reuse` later
requires its exact refusal. Successful actors use independently enrolled
canonical logins, sources and accounts. The shared-seat regression must fail
before Stop. The item-owned child still exits through relay mail and exercises
the same-seat late-stop/refresh path in `stopcont.py`.

Pass --egress-anthropic to enroll anthropic-api (api.anthropic.com, TCP 443)
and delegate its issued network grant only to the Claudock lane. Codex keeps
an empty network grant list. The protected bundle assembler adds the endpoint
and approved root capability before hashing the publication; run.py binds the
lane only to the member ID actually returned by grants.root. The stand-in
executable and credential fixtures are unchanged. This option alone makes no
authenticated API request and supplies no credential.

The disposable root harness requires iproute2 and nftables, enables IPv4
forwarding for this run, and restores its previous value after unit shutdown.
The installed helper requires CAP_NET_ADMIN for veth/routing/nftables and
CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE for private TCP/UDP DNS on port 53. A missing tool,
forwarding prerequisite or rule transaction fails networked launch. Every
lane retains its own network namespace; no host-network fallback exists.

The helper resolves only the enrolled hostname through its trusted resolver.
It rejects a complete answer containing any private, reserved, link-local,
loopback or host address, including mixed safe/unsafe answers. The host's
interface addresses are inventoried on every refresh. Administrators with
public NAT addresses absent from interfaces include those addresses in the
endpoint's host_addresses inventory in their protected launch enrollment.
Supplied --boot bundles must already contain the reviewed endpoint and root
capability; the harness verifies their scope and preserves their exact bytes.

The namespace receives a sealed, read-only /etc/resolv.conf pointing to its
private resolver, which answers only the enrolled name from the current IPv4
firewall pins. TCP and UDP DNS work; other names are refused, and AAAA queries
receive no addresses. IPv6 traffic is denied. Both original and post-DNAT
destinations must match the current pins on TCP 443. Host INPUT, other ports,
metadata, other public hosts and redirected destinations are denied. Forwarded
TLS stays encrypted end to end; the application validates the server certificate.

DNS answers are pinned to the lease, with a 30-second DNS TTL and a refresh
every 30 seconds. A refresh adds resolved addresses with bounded timeouts
before publishing DNS answers, without flushing existing membership. Prior
addresses expire through their kernel timeouts. Kernel pins expire within
70 seconds even after helper failure,
and never outlive the network grant. Resolution, rebinding or update failure
withdraws the link and refuses further launch under that lease. There is no
automatic reopen; a new authorized lease is required. A grantless lane keeps
an empty private namespace and cannot use these routes or DNS listeners.

## Real Claudock without credentials

On that same authorized disposable Linux box, add --real-claudock to the
default lifecycle command. It cannot be combined with --suite state or an
external --boot bundle. The harness clones laneboss-io/claudock-cli at tag
v0.1.0, builds its actual cmd/claudock, downloads the public Claude installer,
and installs Claude Code 2.1.296 in a new isolated home. It checks both actual
version outputs and measures both executable SHA-256 values. No sign-in or
model request is performed. Public fetches occur only on the box.

The protected runtime adds basename objects claudock and claude, each mode
0555 and digest-bound. They appear as /runtime/claudock and /runtime/claude.
The launch bundle selects the real wrapper only for claudock-cli; the Codex
executable, model and stand-in path stay unchanged. Its existing fixture
descriptor still describes only stand-in bytes and passes the same strict
fixture loader. The initial real profile has metadata and an unavailable
observation, with no credential material. No stand-in authenticator is used
for that profile. Version, download, object-size and publication failures are
still failures; the existing 64 MiB object bound is never relaxed.

If provisioning and the earlier installed gates pass, the first expected stop
is credential selection/acquisition for lane.claudock.start. Keep and report
the actual first failed check, owner and code; a credential refusal is not a
passing lifecycle run. The installed runner now measures the exact vendor's
declared version and credential selection with `--version` and `auth status
--json`, using a fresh private home and synthetic input. The native helper
measures its actual bootstrap under the allocated UID and confinement, then
performs home audits and sealing inside its existing credential-worker boundary.
Wrong pins, missing bootstrap measurement and foreign home identities still
refuse. These checks do not establish provider authentication or a model result;
the separate installed lifecycle and credential-persistence gates still apply.

The runner emits InnerSandbox=off, retaining the full Linux outer validator.
Its only selectable environment name is the broker's run token name. The
native helper supplies HOME (private lane home), PATH (fixed system/runtime
search), TMPDIR (private tmp), XDG_CACHE_HOME, XDG_CONFIG_HOME and
XDG_STATE_HOME (private lane subdirectories), CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR (private
vendor state), CLAUDOCK_CREDENTIAL_STORE (file backend) and
CLAUDOCK_REGISTRY_DIR (private one-profile registry). None of these path or
backend values comes from the lane or task-secret injection. Reserved-name
checks remain in place; fixed helper names are not added to the selectable
list. XDG_STATE_HOME and both CLAUDOCK path/backend names are additionally
reserved against task-secret overrides, matching the helper's existing fixed
environment. Runtime bytes, source revision, installer hash, versions and first-stop
evidence are retained by the harness; no authenticated AI-service claim follows.
Combine --real-claudock with --egress-anthropic to enroll the real wrapper and
the API network grant in the same generated lifecycle run. Executable and
endpoint pins remain independent; combining the options supplies no credential
and does not authorize a model request.

Add `--canary-api-key` to that pair only on the authorized disposable Linux
box. The harness generates a fresh fake `sk-ant-` value in memory and exposes
it over a private root-authenticated socket to the installed E2E publication
adapter. The adapter checks the shape, stores and reads it through the actual
encrypted backend, and enrolls `claude-api-key` through InstallProfile. The
socket authenticates the installed service UID and executable digest; neither
argv nor a file carries the fake value. This is declared test material, not
proof that a provider accepted a key. All real runner compatibility, bootstrap
and private-home gates still apply and the first refusal stops the run.

The real-key run waits before stop within the smaller admitted/requested wall
budget, measured from the original start command. Its reads and proof commands
share that deadline. A service-origin zero-survivor `run.observed` for the exact
handle permits one `lane.check` to commit the natural `lane.ended` with reason
`exit`; a refusal propagates without retry. The stop row then exercises the
documented ended-run path, verifying its exact end ID, sequence and payload,
and unchanged lane generation/state. It remains a required stop proof.

The fake-key diagnostic reaches `lane.claudock.reply` after the existing stop
and quiescent rows. Quiescent records the actual terminal zero-survivor
observation; reply uses that same run and handle without issuing `lane.check`.
Its 90-second read window begins after quiescence, preserving the full window
for the owner's reads. The service's containment rule is unchanged.
It reads the bound capture through the owner's verified human `blob.read`,
checking each chunk and whole-object hash. The row requires a sealed vendor
result with `is_error: true` and either `api_error_status: 401` or a structured
authentication class: `authentication_failed`, `authentication_error`,
`invalid_api_key` or `token_expired`. A message class must belong to the same
session and capture; quoted reply text supplies no authentication evidence.
Other errors fail with a fixed class name. Spend or wrapper exit alone cannot
pass. Real-key mode requires a successful billed capture result containing the
heading read from the published fixture README. A stopped, fully drained capture
without a vendor result fails immediately with `run stopped before its vendor
result`. The reply read window remains 90 seconds and each command retains its
full ten-second reservation.

The observed row retains `is_error`, `subtype`, `api_error_status`, `num_turns`,
`duration_ms` and `error_class`. Reply command output remains private in memory
with `output_withheld: true`; neither raw reply text nor headers enter that
row. The reader checks `identity.self` against the enrolled human, and the
later collection path preserves this verified row. Key canary checks
and withholding remain unchanged. An unreadable result cannot prove the fake
key's authentication refusal, and the harness never reads the helper's spool
as root. A fake-key refusal is not an authenticated AI-service test or a
successful model result.

After the Claudock lane stops, before collection removes its home, the harness
reads the native helper's exact home binding and scans the home, Claude config,
Claudock registry and credential store. Cleanup also scans helper state, all
three owned unit journals and evidence for the whole key and its last eight
characters. `api-key-canary.json` reports found/not-found and unreadable counts
per place, without values. An absent lane measurement or empty journal fails
the check. Material found in command output is withheld before evidence is
written and remains an exposure failure. The vendor and its children inherit
the key; this test measures any persistence by the real binaries. The API's
refusal of the fake key is expected and does not establish authenticated
AI-service compatibility. Laneboss runs this real-vendor box measurement.

e2e/v2/cases/serve/PREWALK.md covers all 78 lifecycle catalog rows at source
179f3f0. Its source-ready/blocked/unknown labels are static analysis, not runtime
acceptance, and the harness never imports those labels. REQUESTS.md orders the
remaining owner gaps by first affected step, including current session bootstrap,
canonical org validation, root profiles, launch admission and archive/collection.

admin.publication now includes a real org.validate using the committed receipt's
generation. It requires valid=true and an empty problems array. human.session
keeps its actual receipt checks and additionally requires a fresh CLI session
prelude followed by identity.self on that same socket. A separately returned
session ID is never reused as authority. These are additional measured gates;
neither a cached session receipt nor a static prewalk row credits success.

## Input preparation and authority

provision.stage copies the reviewed sources and fixtures to protected root-owned
staging and archives their exact bytes. During boot.inputs, run.py builds the
standalone scripts/v2-serve/bundle assembler from that staged checkout. It runs
the assembler with a protected input file containing the actual runtime bytes,
executable pins, shipped sources, judge pins and a current one-hour expiry. The
assembler is not linked into Laneboss, has no publication client or state writer,
and is never invoked by the installed service. Generation happens in the harness
before installation.

The test org contains:

* The unchanged shipped org prompt, org driver catalog and twelve-entry workflow
  pack from templates/v2/org, plus the shipped L5 supervisor prompt/driver pair.
  All twelve refusal rules apply to the only enabled role, L5 supervisor. There
  are no skip entries, profile exclusions or capture-refusal approval.
* An effective policy whose typed JSON hash is computed from its actual bytes,
  using the real boot judge pins. The fixture judge is an unavailable reserved
  local endpoint; it does not supply a model result or inference approval.
* One project tree containing fixtures/README.md. Its launch base and data grant
  select the exact tree manifest digest and history_scope=tree.
* The actual runtime manifest and content, both declared driver enrollments and
  the actual executable/version pins built by this case. The fixture task has
  its own task-class object; the runtime's task artifact remains a separate pin.
* One rw/copy tree at /work/main and two unpaid, nondelegating serve-fixture
  profile selectors, one per driver. The root manifest has empty resource,
  secret, network and channel arrays. These selectors contain no credential and
  are enrolled by the dedicated fixture build's native administrator publication
  through the real secrets.InstallProfile method. Its generated service evidence
  explicitly records unavailable vendor room. The harness never writes those
  encrypted records, and enrollment creates no credential material or lease.
* A zero-spend budget: wall 60 seconds, CPU 30 seconds, memory 256 MiB, disk
  32 MiB and at most 16 processes. Lane proposals initially have no issued grant
  IDs or root ticket.

The assembler calls the existing policy.ValidateInitial, templates.Resolve,
templates.SeedRules, templates.ValidateInitialSources,
api.ValidateLaunchEnrollment, manifest.Object, manifest.Root and
manifest.RootInput validators. It returns their typed failures. It calculates
object hashes and lengths from the exact bytes; no expected digest is used as a
successful result. The installer still runs its real --validate-initial mode
before staging the publication. Neither product defaults nor relaxed validators
are introduced by the harness.

New initial inputs live below a fresh root-owned 0700 directory under protected
/opt. Every initial descriptor and object is a single-link root-owned 0444
regular file, read back with no-follow metadata checks, exact hashes and byte
counts. The boot, release and assembler input files are 0600. The native key
stays in private provisioning/installed storage and is excluded from evidence.
The default boot supplies the actual initial_publication, initial_objects and
launch_enrollment references/files to the administrator installer.

Evidence retains source.tar, source-files.json, harness.tar, the assembler binary,
bundle-input.json.input, actual command stdout/stderr/exit records, all public
initial-inputs bytes, launch-enrollment.json.input and native input path metadata.
The exact root request and bound launch request are also retained when used.
Protected staging remains on the disposable box until it is collected.

## Publication, session and first launch gates

The initial-bundle path on base a282b26 exposed an installer variable collision:
initial-object iteration replaced the supplied native-key bytes with an object
ID before the private key write. The installer now keeps those bytes in a
separate native_key binding. All administrator input, immutable file, digest and
pairing checks remain required. REQUESTS.md in the serve E2E case records exact
source references and the remaining administrator version question.

Installation must read back the supplied pins and report publication=pending.
The harness also reads the actual protected initial-validation exit and stdout
under the install evidence. A success must have exit zero, valid=true and the
exact supplied initial manifest/ID; missing, malformed or mismatched evidence
fails installation. Installer failure remains failure even when its earlier
native validation succeeded. A typed native refusal is retained without reading
argv or native-key contents, and no failed install is retried.
After genuine enrollment, the harness calls the real native publication path
twice and requires the same durable receipt, exact operation/event/sequence and
generation. The publisher verifies current policy, snapshot, objects and marker
bytes. Human-session reads independently verify the marker, initial descriptor,
policy bytes and completed operation. A receipt alone never passes this gate.

Those reads invoke the existing human.session.challenge/confirm path. The
software device signs the actual operation-bound challenges; only the installed
identity service can issue proofs and sessions. A challenge refusal stops all
dependent reads and lanes, with the real owner/code and raw evidence retained.

Green11 passed installation and enrollment, then reached a publication-handler
deadline. The service adapter now separates ten seconds of work from an eleven-
second transport lifetime, keeping its bounded refusal-write window after
request cancellation; parent shutdown still closes the transport. The helper's
earlier deadline was repaired by administrator d4fix17's bounded nested relay
contract. Green13 returned the service-owned timeout through that relay. The
remaining publication cost and next-step gaps are in REQUESTS.md; no publication
pass is inferred from error delivery.

Each failing step retains both owned unit journals before unit shutdown, using
exact _SYSTEMD_UNIT or UNIT matches for each installed unit. There is one query per
unit, without a glob or retry. Raw MESSAGE lines, stderr, actual exit, hash,
line count and command elapsed milliseconds are retained. Empty and failed
queries are explicit. Journal context is linked to the first failing step and
printed as separate journal_verdict lines; it cannot replace the original RPC
owner/code. Journal errors still fail the aggregate report. Publication-input
object and byte counts are retained to support the deadline investigation.

Initial publication additionally writes JSON journal records with exactly stage
and elapsed_ms. Fixed initial_publish stages cover the native channel, protected
reads, complete bundle load/validation, object hashing, policy/templates, the
second validation, plan, seal, store commit, each read-back object, full read-back
and total. These are monotonic wall durations. Parent stages include children;
do not sum nested durations or interpret them as per-stage CPU measurements.
Repeated protected_read/read_object records reveal costly work without printing
any input value or object selector. A final total closes each serialized fixture
attempt. Timings do not authorize work or change the ten-second service budget.

Journal capture reads back the saved stdout bytes before accepting collection.
Parsed timing records retain their exact journal line, stdout location and hash
through cleanup and first-failure restoration. The summary emits initial_timing
lines even on a successful run. Unknown stages, extra fields, invalid durations
or missing required timing fail collection; no aggregate pass can omit timing
evidence. A journal-write error also remains an actual publication error.
REQUESTS.md records the quadratic whole-object read/hash gap for the store owner;
stage timing must establish whether that stage was reached before attributing
the measured timeout to it. No installed performance or publication pass is
claimed from source inspection.

After a genuine grants.root result, the default harness checks that its actual
profile and data records exactly match the approved fixture scope. It fills the
launch proposal only with the returned member IDs. It then obtains a separately
issued ticket and calls the real lane.start. It never invents a grant, ticket,
publication receipt or lane success. Lifecycle still requires the actual
operation, process, status, events and collection read-backs.

The current source has unresolved product boundaries; no installed pass is
claimed by assembling these inputs:

* Integrate fix 13 resolves the kind rule: Sources stay semantic, Derived uses
  file and contains real generated output, and manifest-tree entries use file.
  Full templates validation and the initial empty-Derived gate still need to
  adopt that coordinated rule. The fixture preserves semantic source bytes;
  templates.Resolve is not a replacement for full installed org.validate.
* Full org validation requests the current snapshot without selectors, whereas
  the store currently requires explicit org/generation selectors. Source,
  TemplateSources and BriefingInput remain service-owned unimplemented ports.
* grants.root currently returns lb-v2-grants not_implemented for nonempty profile
  selections pending a protected enrollment resolver. The fixture manifest is
  inert input, not a replacement for that resolver. Root/member profile IDs also
  need the grants/lifecycle contract to agree with reservation-derived IDs.
* Native credential/endpoint/worker projections and later lifecycle, capture and
  recovery ports still require their owners' real implementations and Linux
  controls. Unreached assertions remain FAIL/unrun.

## Explicit external bundle

The stand-in fixture retains its request files, partial prompt and transcript
at /home/lane/serve-state in the lane's private home mount. Its enrollment
descriptor pins that exact output path, and recorded CLI request paths are
absolute within the same home. Normal completion, cancellation and hard kill
therefore leave the project worktree untouched by fixture scratch. The normal
archive retains private-home evidence. No worktree ignore rule or dirty-work
cleanup exception is installed; real agent work remains subject to the same
retention gate.

--config and --boot remain a paired alternative. --boot names a complete protected
administrator boot bundle as documented in scripts/v2-admin/README.md. Its exact
publication, source and runtime bytes are consumed unchanged, and must match the
actual trimpath builds, toolchain, dependencies and tags. In this mode, provide
--root-grants and --lane-inputs for lifecycle, or --root-grants and --launch-request
for state. The harness never repairs a rejected input or repins it to pass.

Default installed mode requires no extra run.py arguments. Runtime acceptance of
the new bundle, human.session.challenge and first lane.start must be measured by
the next authorized Linux box run.
